&+ \sum_{j = 1}^K\bigl(\lambda\,\widehat{t}_j-1\bigr)_+\left|\eta_j\right|
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Here $(x)_+=\max(x,0)$ and $\widehat{t}_j$ is the standardized discrepancy (Wald) statistic between the source-assisted and target-only estimators,
\[
\widehat{t}_j
\;=\;
\frac{\bigl|\widehat{\mu}^1_{ot}-\widehat{\mu}^1_{t,s_j}\bigr|}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\mathrm{disc}_j)},
\qquad
\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\mathrm{disc}_j)^2
\;=\;
\frac{\widehat{V}_{ot}}{N_t}
+\frac{\widehat{V}_{t,s_j}}{N_t}
+\frac{\widehat{V}_{s_j}}{N_{s_j}}
-\frac{2\,\widehat{C}_{ot,s_j}}{N_t}
\;=\;
\widehat{\Var}\bigl(\widehat{\mu}^1_{t,s_j}-\widehat{\mu}^1_{ot}\bigr).
\]
This truncated-Wald penalty replaces the raw squared discrepancy $(\widehat{\mu}^1_{ot}-\widehat{\mu}^1_{t,s_j})^2$ used in the original adaptive-aggregation objective, and is the finite-sample selection remedy of FACE \citep[Remark~9]{han2025federated}: the $\ell_1$ coefficient on $|\eta_j|$ is $0$ (source $s_j$ kept at its unpenalized, variance-minimizing coordinate) when $\widehat{t}_j\le 1/\lambda$, and positive (source $s_j$ shrunk toward zero) when $\widehat{t}_j>1/\lambda$, so $1/\lambda$ acts as a Wald inclusion threshold.

Because $\widehat{t}_j$ is self-normalized, the penalty does not require any variance-scaling absorption: the $\sqrt{N_{\mathrm{all}}}$ rate already enters through the statistic itself, with $\widehat{t}_j=\Theta_p(\sqrt{N_{\mathrm{all}}}\,b_j)\to\infty$ for a biased source whose separation $b_j>0$ and $\widehat{t}_j=O_p(1)$ for an informative source. Consequently the aggregation rate condition is $\lambda\to 0$ and $\lambda\sqrt{N_{\mathrm{all}}}\to\infty$ (equivalently the inclusion threshold $1/\lambda\to\infty$ but slower than $\sqrt{N_{\mathrm{all}}}$), as used in the Supplemental Material.

    \item Evaluate $Q_{\mathrm{val},k_2}(\lambda)$ by plugging $\widehat{\boldsymbol{\eta}}^{(-k_1,-k_2)}(\lambda)$ into the validation penalized criterion
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lambda_validation_loss_app}
Q_{\mathrm{val},k_2}(\lambda)
=
N_{\mathrm{val},k_2}\,
\widehat{\Var}_{\mathrm{val},k_2}\!\left\{\widehat{\boldsymbol{\eta}}^{(-k_1,-k_2)}(\lambda)\right\}
+\sum_{j=1}^K\bigl(\lambda\,\widehat{t}^{\,\mathrm{val}}_{j,(k_1,k_2)}-1\bigr)_+
\left|\widehat{\eta}^{(-k_1,-k_2)}_j(\lambda)\right|,
\end{equation}
with $\widehat{t}^{\,\mathrm{val}}_{j,(k_1,k_2)}$ the validation-fold Wald statistic of \cref{eq:final_opt}. This optional criterion is used only if one tunes $\lambda$ over a grid; the default implementation instead fixes $1/\lambda$ at a Wald significance level and skips this inner-fold loop.

supp_edits.old_string:
The penalized objective from \maincref{eq:final_opt} is:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:penalized_obj}
\widehat{\eta} = \arg\min_\eta \left\{ \widehat{L}(\eta) + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^K |\eta_j| \left( \widehat{\mu}^1_{ot} - \widehat{\mu}^1_{t,s_j} \right)^2 \right\}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda > 0$ is a tuning parameter that depends on $N_{\mathrm{all}}$.

The penalty term $(\widehat{\mu}^1_{ot} - \widehat{\mu}^1_{t,s_j})^2$ measures the discrepancy between the target-only and source-assisted estimators. Large discrepancies indicate potential covariate shift or model misspecification, and the penalty drives the corresponding $\eta_j$ toward zero.
